      "content": "Summary",
      "id": "7a33b23f-9074-41e2-aa93-f7a731ccbfa1"
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": "Before a noun, an adjective gets -e (Part I rule); a singular het-word after een/geen is the exception.",
      "id": "951eeb3f-e48f-4734-99fb-85011c2807f5"
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": "After zijn or worden, the adjective never gets -e: De dag is koud. Het wordt warm.",
      "id": "fdf1a42f-efd9-49ec-9f63-69725134897f"
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": "As an adverb (how something happens), the word is always the base form: Het waait hard. Daan loopt snel.",
      "id": "d2d903be-b01f-41d1-9a66-60e6a876508d"
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": "heel, erg, best, een beetje and te go right before the adjective or adverb and never change themselves: heel koud, een beetje nat, te druk. best means quite/fairly, not \"best\".",
      "id": "c993006f-ea58-41cb-955f-b5ce5a194cd0"
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": "lekker is both adjective and adverb and never takes an ending: lekker weer, lekker warm, Ik slaap lekker.",
      "id": "9ac4178e-ffec-4ee4-8e64-8f5b135e93b9"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        "Remember: ",
        {
          "text": "Het waait hard.",
